WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:14.530
CREATIVITY: IT’S AT THE HEART OF WHO WE
HUMANS ARE…

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:25.190
…WITH BRAINS EQUIPPED BY EVOLUTION TO INVENT,
TO WRITE, TO MAKE ART AND MUSIC.

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:29.410
WE HUMANS ARE SPECIAL, RIGHT?

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.450
BUT WAIT.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:35.620
WHAT IF A ROBOT COULD PAINT A MASTERPIECE?

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:45.020
WHAT IF ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE COULD COMPOSE
GREAT MUSIC?

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:51.110
DOES A ROBOT – A MACHINE WITH ARTIFICIAL
INTELLIGENCE – DRIVEN BY CODE AND ALGORITHMS

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:56.309
-- FEEL AND EXPRESS ITSELF UNIQUELY AND ORIGINALLY?

00:00:56.309 --> 00:01:03.170
You’re just a machine, an imitation of life.

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:06.040
Can a robot write a symphony?

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:10.600
Can a robot turn a canvas into a beautiful
masterpiece?

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:17.170
Can you?

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:23.670
OVER SOME 40,000 YEARS, HUMAN CREATIVITY HAS
EXPLODED – FROM DRAWINGS ON CAVE WALLS THROUGH

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.210
THE GREAT ART OF CENTURIES TO COME….

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:33.859
TO EVER MORE ADVANCED TECHNOLOGY AND DESIGN.

00:01:33.859 --> 00:01:44.289
NOW, SCIENTISTS -- AND ARTISTS --ARE ASKING
CAN A ROBOT TRULY IMAGINE AN ORIGINAL MASTERWORK?

00:01:44.289 --> 00:01:47.600
OR CAN IT ONLY COPY THE PATTERNS OF A HUMAN
PAINTER…

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:48.920
OR MUSICIAN?

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:59.310
DOES THIS COMPUTER VERSION OF VIVALDI SOUND
LIKE THE REAL THING?

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:04.299
CAN ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE FEEL EMOTION AND
TURN IT INTO ART?

00:02:04.299 --> 00:02:11.360
DO ALGORITHMS WITH YES-NO OPTIONS HAVE THE
POTENTIAL FOR UNLIMITED CREATIVITY?

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:20.600
EVEN THE HUMAN BRAIN, WITH ITS 86 BILLION
NEURONS, MAY HAVE CREATIVE LIMITS.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:25.319
COMPUTATIONAL CREATIVITY IS LEADING US TO
ASK NEW QUESTIONS ABOUT HUMAN CREATIVITY.

00:02:25.319 --> 00:02:29.640
IS THIS ESSENTIAL HUMAN TRAIT TRULY UNIQUE?

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.810
HOW FAR CAN IT TAKE US?

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:34.959
WILL ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE BE A COMPETITOR?

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:42.810
OR CAN IT BE A COLLABORATOR, HELPING US TOWARD
STILL UNIMAGINED CREATIONS?

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:53.810
Hello and welcome to Computational Creativity.

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:59.239
My first guest is a member of Google Brain’s
Magenta team.

00:02:59.239 --> 00:03:04.720
He is currently working on neural network
models of sound and music and recently produced

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:08.459
a synthesizer that designed its own sounds.

00:03:08.459 --> 00:03:10.500
He’s also a jazz guitarist.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:18.180
Please welcome Jesse Engel.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:23.790
Also with us, is an Assistant professor at
the University of Illinois at Urbana Champaign

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:27.189
in the Dept. of Electrical and Computer Engineering.

00:03:27.189 --> 00:03:34.150
He focuses on several surprising creative
domains including the culinary arts and fashion

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:36.610
and the theoretical foundations of creativity.

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:44.049
Please welcome Lav Varshney

00:03:44.049 --> 00:03:48.950
Also with us is an Associate Professor of
psychological and brain science at Dartmouth

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:49.950
College.

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:55.319
He’s interested in the neural basis of imagination
and in the evolution of human creativity.

00:03:55.319 --> 00:04:03.420
Please welcome Peter Tse.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:08.640
And finally, an artist in residence at Bell
Labs exploring new forms of drawing with virtual

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.159
reality, biometrics, machines learning and
robotics.

00:04:12.159 --> 00:04:17.631
A former research fellow at MIT’s Media
lab and artist in residence at Google, please

00:04:17.631 --> 00:04:23.920
welcome Sougwen Chung.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:33.160
Peter, it seems like there are many possible
pros and cons for approaching computational

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.850
creativity.

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:42.260
One potential con would be we don’t even
fully understand human creativity yet.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:49.020
As someone who has studied the evolution of
human creativity, where does it come from,

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:52.330
what do we know about it, what don’t we
know about it?

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:59.440
So we’re mammals that have descended from
a very long lineage and in the hominin lineage,

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:04.380
we diverged from chimpanzees something like
seven million years ago, from the common ancestors

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:07.800
with chimpanzees and bonobos about seven million
years ago.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:15.880
And we went through a long period of having
small brains, we were bipedal, the australopithecines

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:20.350
which you can see in the lower left there
and the lower middle.

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:29.380
But then around 2.2 million years ago, homo
habilis created stone tools and these Olduvai

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:33.130
stone tools were very simple, basically just
broken rocks.

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:41.220
But then about 1.8 million, 2.0 million years
ago, homo erectus in the upper middle there

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:46.370
created a kind of hand axe called the Acheulean
hand axe which hardly changed for a million

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:47.370
and a half years.

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:51.950
So it’s really striking is how un-innovative
homo erectus was…

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:59.590
Had one idea and stuck with it.

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:05.750
And then heidelbergensis, which is in the
upper right there is about six hundred to

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:07.130
eight hundred thousand years ago.

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:15.520
Again, very non innovative and even the Neanderthals
who had the so-called Mousterian tool set,

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:18.650
it hardly changed for over one hundred fifty
thousand years.

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:23.940
And then our species shows up, and in the
case of Europe kind of in the middle of Europe

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:31.030
probably came up the Danube and couldn’t
go any further and their artifacts are incredible,

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:38.620
you see the first evidence of instruments,
of art and we can know for sure how this came

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:43.590
about because thoughts don’t leave fossils,
neuro-circuits don’t leave fossils, all

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:48.090
we have are bones and skulls and artifacts.

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:52.520
But the artifacts that show up are just incredible;
stone flutes…

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:56.440
I mean bone flutes and beautiful art.

00:06:56.440 --> 00:07:01.340
So something happened in our neural circuitry
that afforded us the capacity to think of

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:07.860
such things and then paint them or create
them.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:15.450
And some of them involve the creation of objects
that existed in their world like that bison

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:21.110
up there, but some of them involve the creation
of objects that could not exist.

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:27.690
And near Un in what is now Germany, there’s
a cave where they found this Löwenmensch,

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:29.280
this lion headed human.

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:35.880
So there was never a lion headed human, somebody
had to imagine that, thirty-five to forty

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:39.190
thousand years ago and then go and build it.

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:44.140
I’m very interested in the neural circuitry
that could have created that.

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:48.390
Just to be clear, this is not a result of
some kind of physical evolution, the brain

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:54.510
pan doesn’t get… we don’t see an increase
in the size of the brain in the species.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:59.960
No we do see an increase in the size of the
brain from roughly the size of a chimpanzee,

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:04.290
three hundred fifty cubic centimeters, up
through homo habilis getting up to maybe six

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:11.660
hundred CC, homo erectus gets bigger and bigger
and the Neanderthals actually had brains that

00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:16.690
were bigger than ours, much bigger, well not
much bigger maybe fourteen hundred, fourteen-fifty

00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:19.730
CC whereas ours is in the thirteen hundreds.

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:24.660
And yet Neanderthals with their bigger brains
were not very innovative, their tools hardly

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:25.660
changed.

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:32.459
But what really seems to change in our skulls
and in our brains, because the skull molds

00:08:32.459 --> 00:08:36.710
to fit the brain… is this, our foreheads
got really big.

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:43.800
So something about our frontal cortex changed
and I suspect this is central to our becoming

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:46.009
more creative.

00:08:46.009 --> 00:08:51.120
So when did this happen, I mean homo sapiens
as a species was around for a while and it

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:57.589
seems like what you’re saying, it sort of
describes a J curve where there’s a sudden

00:08:57.589 --> 00:08:58.839
explosion of…

00:08:58.839 --> 00:09:03.569
It’s a big puzzle right, so I can look at
a skull from one hundred-thirty thousand years

00:09:03.569 --> 00:09:06.390
ago and it looks more or less like a modern
skull.

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:12.830
But the first evidence of sort of jewelry
and art is maybe seventy, maybe eighty-thousand

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:15.029
years ago in South Africa in the Blombos Cave.

00:09:15.029 --> 00:09:20.680
And in Europe, Cro-Magnon man our species
shows up about forty-one thousand years ago

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:22.290
give or take a couple thousand of years.

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:25.899
And it really seemed to be a big bang, a cultural
big bang.

00:09:25.899 --> 00:09:27.800
And what that was, I think nobody knows.

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:34.069
Was it a change in culture, a change in language?

00:09:34.069 --> 00:09:35.069
Nobody knows.

00:09:35.069 --> 00:09:38.139
Lav you’ve studied some of the theoretical
foundations of creativity.

00:09:38.139 --> 00:09:42.290
It is important to at least ask these questions.

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:47.639
Because if we’re going to talk about computational
creativity, we need to know what we’re starting

00:09:47.639 --> 00:09:48.639
from.

00:09:48.639 --> 00:09:53.519
Exactly, and so what I’ve been working on
is a mathematical theory of creativity which

00:09:53.519 --> 00:09:58.829
again seems perhaps strange, how can you mathematize
something like creativity?

00:09:58.829 --> 00:10:03.000
And the way I’ve been defining it is, we
want things that are both novel as well as

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.430
high quality in the domain.

00:10:04.430 --> 00:10:09.480
So if we’re thinking about food, we want
it to be flavorful or if we want fashion,

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:12.060
we want it to be visually appealing perhaps.

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:15.259
But novelty seems to be fairly consistent
across domains.

00:10:15.259 --> 00:10:19.689
Things that change your beliefs are perhaps
the most surprising and the most novel.

00:10:19.689 --> 00:10:27.870
The human brain as neuroscientist know, has
balanced the need for knowing what’s coming

00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:34.740
next, expectation, the satisfaction the brain
gets when it knows the next digit in the sequence

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:40.110
or the next word in the sentence versus novelty.

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:47.029
Whether you can mathematically determine that
sweet spot is still kind of an open question

00:10:47.029 --> 00:10:48.029
I would imagine.

00:10:48.029 --> 00:10:49.029
It is, yeah.

00:10:49.029 --> 00:10:53.759
How to balance novelty against familiarity
that’s really the question that artists

00:10:53.759 --> 00:10:54.759
face.

00:10:54.759 --> 00:10:59.350
You don’t want to deviate too much perhaps
because then its not understandable but you

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:00.749
definitely want to push the envelope.

00:11:00.749 --> 00:11:07.629
So with food, does the computer put together
things that no human chef would ever dream

00:11:07.629 --> 00:11:12.370
of putting together, like blueberries and
pork?

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:16.839
Yeah, you can definitely come up with novel
combinations that have never been done before

00:11:16.839 --> 00:11:24.660
so things like cocoa powder and saffron and
almonds for example, that’s incredibly good

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:32.360
Now…you’ve also been working with an algorithm
that...if we’re going to talk about artificial

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:34.779
intelligence learning.

00:11:34.779 --> 00:11:42.420
There are the different algorithms and before
we get to Jesse and his musical tools.

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:48.339
Talk us through this algorithm that helps
learn a machine to learn to create music.

00:11:48.339 --> 00:11:53.850
Right so what we wanted to do was essentially
learn what are the laws of music theory?

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:57.779
What are the principles of good music as it
were?

00:11:57.779 --> 00:12:06.620
So what this algorithm is doing is it has
two parts.

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:12.249
Essentially a teacher that knows Bach and
a student who is trying to learn what is music.

00:12:12.249 --> 00:12:16.089
And they iterate between each other
So we want to build this hierarchy of concepts

00:12:16.089 --> 00:12:19.490
in music, and further we want them to be human
interruptible.

00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:23.990
So that’s roughly what this algorithm tries
to do and then eventually, we can use those

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:28.529
principles then to inform composition and
the generative question of creativity.

00:12:28.529 --> 00:12:34.970
So is there an analogy to how we as humans
would learn the theory of making music?

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:40.560
Yeah in fact the rules that the algorithm
generates are exactly or nearly the ones that

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:42.009
are taught in music theory courses.

00:12:42.009 --> 00:12:46.470
They go step by step in roughly the same way
though there are a couple of rules that come

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:50.769
out that some of our colleagues and colleagues
of music find kind of interesting as well,

00:12:50.769 --> 00:12:52.130
things that they hadn’t noticed.

00:12:52.130 --> 00:12:58.279
Now Jesse in the field of music we have seen
some attempts made a quote unquote generated

00:12:58.279 --> 00:12:59.300
music.

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:03.399
Brian Eno for example in England has been
pursuing this for years.

00:13:03.399 --> 00:13:10.899
What’s the attraction in taking something
so inherently human and trying to expand that

00:13:10.899 --> 00:13:13.279
into the world of artificial intelligence?

00:13:13.279 --> 00:13:15.949
That’s a great question.

00:13:15.949 --> 00:13:21.110
So I think it’s interesting to think of
things in the context that creativity and

00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:24.329
tools have always been co-involving.

00:13:24.329 --> 00:13:30.540
We weren’t able to… you know the evolution
of well tempered instruments allowed for playing

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:37.370
in multiple keys, algorithm and machine learning,
artificial intelligence are just sort of the

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:43.930
next step in this process where they’re
creating new opportunities to interact with

00:13:43.930 --> 00:13:53.430
composition, new ways to interact with sonic
texture and just new tools to express ourselves

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:54.630
creatively.

00:13:54.630 --> 00:14:01.089
What are you and the Magenta team at Google,
what is your kind of approach to the music

00:14:01.089 --> 00:14:02.850
creativity process?

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:09.319
What’s interesting about machine learning
and what’s called AI is that it’s really

00:14:09.319 --> 00:14:15.259
a data driven way or at least a lot of the
current methods are a data driven way of discovering

00:14:15.259 --> 00:14:22.540
some of these core components to a given set
of data, whether it be audio from someone

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:27.819
talking or the core components of a recipe
or anything like that.

00:14:27.819 --> 00:14:33.230
And so once you’ve identified these core
components, then you can start playing games

00:14:33.230 --> 00:14:35.949
about how can I mix and match them?

00:14:35.949 --> 00:14:41.290
The systems aren’t perfect, they fail in
interesting ways because they’re trying

00:14:41.290 --> 00:14:44.730
to do the best they can, and they’re trying
to do it according to the data we show them

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:48.050
and the data that we show them comes from
the natural world.

00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:52.290
So the mistakes they make are somewhat in
line with the natural world.

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:56.829
And so they have a kind of more humanistic
character to them.

00:14:56.829 --> 00:15:00.450
Which is actually a great source of, it’s
a different kind of creativity.

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:04.470
This is creativity through trial and error
you know, or mistakes.

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:07.089
Well that sounds very human.

00:15:07.089 --> 00:15:11.790
Jamaican musicians in the early 60’s listening
to offshore radio from the southern American

00:15:11.790 --> 00:15:16.220
states and thinking I’ve got to do me some
of that R&amp;B stuff and getting it wrong and

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:19.529
creating reggae.

00:15:19.529 --> 00:15:25.259
This is how almost every kind of development
in our arts has happened.

00:15:25.259 --> 00:15:31.790
Or like in the case of, that the 808 drum
machine was originally invented to be a perfectly

00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:34.800
accurate drummer, it was supposed to be a
replacement of a drummer.

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:39.180
But it wasn’t, it sounds like a drum in
a box.

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:45.300
But it actually the way that it was not perfect
with the kick drum and stuff was actually

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:50.619
the starting point for a lot of electronic
music and hip-hop.

00:15:50.619 --> 00:15:55.129
So there’s the way that you have your creative
intention and it goes a little bit off but

00:15:55.129 --> 00:15:59.540
if you’re guiding your creative intention,
if it’s grounded in the world, it can be

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:01.949
a great creative source as well.

00:16:01.949 --> 00:16:08.319
With these machines, is the learning experiential,
can it be experiential?

00:16:08.319 --> 00:16:14.899
Or is it completely based on what we the people
have taught it?

00:16:14.899 --> 00:16:21.279
I definitely think it can be experiential,
the standard way of teaching things is to

00:16:21.279 --> 00:16:28.009
give it a bunch of exemplars, and then it
has to try to… it meaning the algorithm

00:16:28.009 --> 00:16:32.709
that you come up with has to try to determine
some structure within that.

00:16:32.709 --> 00:16:40.749
But there’s an alternative way where you
give a bunch of core components and an algorithm

00:16:40.749 --> 00:16:44.929
can try to combine these components in different
ways.

00:16:44.929 --> 00:16:49.309
Evolutionary algorithms where you sort of
take these two things and you say what if

00:16:49.309 --> 00:16:53.149
I combine them, I would have a child of them.

00:16:53.149 --> 00:16:57.670
There’s also reinforcement learning where
you sort of give it feedback if it combines

00:16:57.670 --> 00:17:01.839
two things in a good way, then you say okay
good job and it does more things like that.

00:17:01.839 --> 00:17:07.110
And those are some of the frontiers of these
types of algorithms.

00:17:07.110 --> 00:17:12.709
At the table there you have a kind of melody
generating system that puts this theory into

00:17:12.709 --> 00:17:13.709
practice right?

00:17:13.709 --> 00:17:19.440
Right, so the Google Magenta project is part
of a machine learning research group.

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:25.620
So we do fundamental research, but one of
the key things I think often gets overlooked

00:17:25.620 --> 00:17:28.000
is there’s always a human in the creative
process.

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:33.630
So it’s an open source project that we try
to get as much engagement from creative coders

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:35.230
and artist in the community.

00:17:35.230 --> 00:17:40.400
And we try to create tools that allow these
artists to interact with these algorithms

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:45.110
without having to do a bunch of programing
on the command line and that type of stuff.

00:17:45.110 --> 00:17:47.850
And so I just brought along a few of them
here to show.

00:17:47.850 --> 00:17:51.680
Why don’t you demonstrate what one of these
can do?

00:17:51.680 --> 00:17:58.420
Sougwen, while Jesse’s walking over to the
table, does this sound analogous to your sort

00:17:58.420 --> 00:17:59.660
of collaboration?

00:17:59.660 --> 00:18:08.220
I started a robotic drawing collaboration
a few years ago with the intent of exploring

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:14.430
how we can measure creativity and actually
reflects that back as a type of performance.

00:18:14.430 --> 00:18:21.720
So I think as I come to work with the robotic
arm and with these new machine-learning algorithms,

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:28.980
I’ve come to view it as more of a collaborator
because of its ability to reflect our own

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:30.280
creative processes.

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:34.940
And I think that’s a new process that’s
only going to grow more in the future.

00:18:34.940 --> 00:18:37.050
Now what are we looking at here?

00:18:37.050 --> 00:18:41.820
So this is my project Drawing Operations Unit
Generation One.

00:18:41.820 --> 00:18:47.370
It’s sort of a first very initial foray
into what it would be like to collaborate

00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:50.090
with a robotic agent.

00:18:50.090 --> 00:18:56.780
The first generation is really simple, I wanted
to show a few things, I wanted to show sort

00:18:56.780 --> 00:19:02.140
of a human hand and a robotic arm working
in harmony cause I feel like that image is

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:10.600
really, and that narrative is underserved
in our culture now and also think about how

00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:18.170
relationally and behaviorally the sort of
two organic and robotic units can work together

00:19:18.170 --> 00:19:24.270
to create something beautiful or not and kind
of explore that side of ambiguity.

00:19:24.270 --> 00:19:30.400
And Jesse was talking about, I think you were
saying that for you getting the feedback from

00:19:30.400 --> 00:19:35.810
the machine affects your creative process,
so it becomes a feedback loop.

00:19:35.810 --> 00:19:36.810
Absolutely.

00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:39.570
You did bring one other video for us to watch.

00:19:39.570 --> 00:19:40.570
What’s happening here?

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:45.740
Oh this is just sort of an older installation
work of mine.

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:52.110
I think it hits upon this sort of unique point
in history that we’re in.

00:19:52.110 --> 00:20:00.670
Where a lot of how we work with traditional
mediums and how we engage in music and food

00:20:00.670 --> 00:20:07.780
how that’s being shifted and transmuted
by these different technological systems.

00:20:07.780 --> 00:20:13.870
So this is an installation project that I
did a few years ago combines sort of hand

00:20:13.870 --> 00:20:19.810
made paper craft with computational graphics
to create an environment that’s sort of

00:20:19.810 --> 00:20:21.890
this union of both.

00:20:21.890 --> 00:20:24.020
So it is a genuine collaborative process?

00:20:24.020 --> 00:20:30.680
It’s a hybrid process.

00:20:30.680 --> 00:20:52.650
Let’s take a look.

00:20:52.650 --> 00:20:58.090
So the idea that it’s a hybrid process it
seemed like maybe you were hedging on whether

00:20:58.090 --> 00:21:02.830
it was a genuine collaboration yet, is that
the goal?

00:21:02.830 --> 00:21:04.900
I think definitely that’s the goal for me.

00:21:04.900 --> 00:21:13.140
I’d like to see more people engaged with
you know machine learning, behaviors as part

00:21:13.140 --> 00:21:17.380
of a process they adopt into their own work.

00:21:17.380 --> 00:21:22.120
I think we’re always inching closer and
closer toward true collaboration even with

00:21:22.120 --> 00:21:23.800
human actors.

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:27.620
So there’s a real area to be explored there.

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:29.690
So what do you got over there?

00:21:29.690 --> 00:21:30.690
Okay.

00:21:30.690 --> 00:21:37.210
So what I have here is a demonstration a very
common type of model in machine learning,

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:43.580
where there’s-given a series of notes, it
looks for structure within those notes and

00:21:43.580 --> 00:21:49.300
tries to keep on continuing and predicting
what would I play next if I was the one who

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:51.360
had played those notes.

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:54.580
And so there’s a lot of different ways you
can use that within a creative process and

00:21:54.580 --> 00:22:00.160
so we’ve taken tens of thousands of popular
songs and just the notes from the songs, its

00:22:00.160 --> 00:22:05.070
called midi data, but we train these models
to find the structure there and then to be

00:22:05.070 --> 00:22:11.110
able to generate new sounds when conditioned
on-when listening to what it is someone is

00:22:11.110 --> 00:22:12.110
playing.

00:22:12.110 --> 00:22:18.500
So if sound works out here… just to keep
me at all centered I’m going to put a metronome

00:22:18.500 --> 00:22:24.990
on… but I can do something where I might
have a melody that I’m going to play into,

00:22:24.990 --> 00:22:29.780
and you’ll hear the electric piano, will
be me, then I’m going to hear a response

00:22:29.780 --> 00:22:35.100
from the algorithm running on my computer
here that will be trying to continue what

00:22:35.100 --> 00:22:39.460
I am playing, it will be a cello… might
go something simple

00:22:39.460 --> 00:22:56.620
It might respond

00:22:56.620 --> 00:23:00.012
So there’s some continuity there with what
I played before.

00:23:00.012 --> 00:23:04.601
I have it looping actually, so you can hear
the response over and over again.

00:23:04.601 --> 00:23:09.460
And you can hear some similarities of what
I played, but its not doing the job of trying

00:23:09.460 --> 00:23:13.580
to replicate what I play, it’s trying to
continue it in a meaningful way.

00:23:13.580 --> 00:23:16.260
It’s done a little inversion of some of
the…

00:23:16.260 --> 00:23:19.770
… yea a little inversion, but you see it’s
sort of keeping to the same key and stuff

00:23:19.770 --> 00:23:25.590
so if I then wanted to go and play chords
behind it and sort of say that sounds real

00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:34.070
good as like a melody for my song maybe I
can build off that and be like…

00:23:34.070 --> 00:23:42.820
… … something like that and all of a sudden
I have the start of course a very popular

00:23:42.820 --> 00:23:44.310
hit song.

00:23:44.310 --> 00:23:52.310
But that’s just sort of one type of way
that you can be using these tools.

00:23:52.310 --> 00:23:55.690
and another type of way is the sounds themselves
right?

00:23:55.690 --> 00:24:00.470
We know which instruments sounds like because
we made all of them.

00:24:00.470 --> 00:24:04.790
But there are potentially a gazillion other
sounds out there.

00:24:04.790 --> 00:24:09.790
So we have to take a whole bunch of individual
instruments and we have it learn the structure

00:24:09.790 --> 00:24:14.650
between all those different instruments and
then provide that as a tool to a musician

00:24:14.650 --> 00:24:18.500
so that you can interpolate between different
instruments and create sounds that are sort

00:24:18.500 --> 00:24:21.250
of novel and new.

00:24:21.250 --> 00:24:25.080
And yes, so it’s sort of a similar idea
but applied now to tambour and texture.

00:24:25.080 --> 00:24:32.300
So like in this example, we can take the sound
originally of a flute.

00:24:32.300 --> 00:24:33.520
Sounds like a flute.

00:24:33.520 --> 00:24:38.800
Here’s the model’s best attempts to replicate
that sound.

00:24:38.800 --> 00:24:46.250
And you notice it sounds more or less like
a flute but it messes up in some ways that

00:24:46.250 --> 00:24:49.740
there’s this upper high frequency content
stuff.

00:24:49.740 --> 00:24:54.390
But its actually like I was saying those are
the kind of mistakes we enjoy.

00:24:54.390 --> 00:25:00.320
Its sort of like you’re running back in
the old days the 60’s people would run,

00:25:00.320 --> 00:25:03.500
turn up their tune amplifiers and get this
upper harmonic distortion.

00:25:03.500 --> 00:25:06.390
And so it’s sort of a natural type of distortion.

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:14.460
But you can take on the other side for novelty
sake let’s take a cat.

00:25:14.460 --> 00:25:20.590
My wife loves cats so she was like you have
to do a cat… so here’s the model, it was

00:25:20.590 --> 00:25:24.530
only trained on instruments like guitars and
flutes, this is it’s best attempts to recreate

00:25:24.530 --> 00:25:25.530
a cat.

00:25:25.530 --> 00:25:29.810
You know it sort of sounds like a cat, but
it’s like if the cat was played by something

00:25:29.810 --> 00:25:30.810
else.

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:36.000
So if you take the flute and the cat and you
were just to add the numbers corresponding

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:41.030
to those waves together it would just sound
like the cat and the flute in the same room.

00:25:41.030 --> 00:25:45.600
You know, there’s two separate sound sources
but instead, if we take those core components

00:25:45.600 --> 00:25:49.900
that the network learns and we mix those core
components together then we get something

00:25:49.900 --> 00:25:54.110
that is a new sound that combines elements
of the two sounds together.

00:25:54.110 --> 00:25:59.810
So it sort of sounds like a flute that meows.

00:25:59.810 --> 00:26:07.790
And I can then turn that into an instrument,
that I can play and of course it’s very

00:26:07.790 --> 00:26:09.910
fun in novelty.

00:26:09.910 --> 00:26:15.940
But we also want to encourage more that just
novelty, you know the creation of new... not

00:26:15.940 --> 00:26:19.960
serious but personal expression.

00:26:19.960 --> 00:26:27.270
So we’ve also created these tools and integrated
them into modern musical work flows as well.

00:26:27.270 --> 00:26:31.050
Lav is there anything similar to that in…

00:26:31.050 --> 00:26:35.530
Yeah so once you have these components you
can start playing around with them even in

00:26:35.530 --> 00:26:37.650
other domains say like culinary.

00:26:37.650 --> 00:26:45.590
You can say I want Asian flavored ceviche
using dairy products, so then you’ll have

00:26:45.590 --> 00:26:49.000
an algorithm that’ll generate something
pretty flavorful and pretty good.

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:53.290
So you can set up… and I think this is probably
true for you Jesse as well, once you start

00:26:53.290 --> 00:26:57.230
using these things you kind of get a sense
for what input parameters are good as well

00:26:57.230 --> 00:27:00.770
so you learn how the tool works and you can
make it do better things.

00:27:00.770 --> 00:27:06.150
And I think actually building the computational
creativity system gives you some insight into

00:27:06.150 --> 00:27:07.600
that.

00:27:07.600 --> 00:27:12.930
A great example is this was build with a data
set that we created and released to the open

00:27:12.930 --> 00:27:17.130
public, was built off sample libraries and
the data set was really biased to trumpets,

00:27:17.130 --> 00:27:18.940
synthesizers and stuff.

00:27:18.940 --> 00:27:22.440
So it does, it sort of makes, like when I
play a piano through it, it doesn’t get

00:27:22.440 --> 00:27:25.270
it just right and it kind of makes it sound
more like a trumpet.

00:27:25.270 --> 00:27:29.411
And so there’s this even in the training
of a model, there’s creative inputs in the

00:27:29.411 --> 00:27:34.150
terms of what is the data that you want it
to represent in these types of things.

00:27:34.150 --> 00:27:39.730
So Sougwen let’s talk about the visual arts,
there has to be an analogy to be made there.

00:27:39.730 --> 00:27:45.900
Well yeah, I was actually thinking about what’s
fascinating about Lav and Jesse’s work and

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:50.520
I think it’s that it takes place in the
site of ambiguity.

00:27:50.520 --> 00:27:57.210
Like we know that when we train these sort
of new models of thinking to do things that

00:27:57.210 --> 00:28:01.930
we already understand it’s kind of boring,
but when it’s to invent a new recipe or

00:28:01.930 --> 00:28:07.150
if it’s to invent a new sound it becomes
a lot more compelling.

00:28:07.150 --> 00:28:08.960
You found the cat/flute compelling?

00:28:08.960 --> 00:28:14.130
I mean well that’s the thing it’s ambiguous
and it’s novel but I think there are ways

00:28:14.130 --> 00:28:19.220
to create our own new narratives using these
tools.

00:28:19.220 --> 00:28:28.770
Like instead of a cat and flute, I can have
a recording of my father’s voice and my

00:28:28.770 --> 00:28:30.210
favorite song, the melody.

00:28:30.210 --> 00:28:35.480
So suddenly I can hybridize those things it
becomes a very meaningful way of engaging

00:28:35.480 --> 00:28:39.550
with each other and like the historical past.

00:28:39.550 --> 00:28:45.210
I think while there’s a lot of novelty…
like I like a cat/flute, I’d love to play

00:28:45.210 --> 00:28:46.210
a cat/flute.

00:28:46.210 --> 00:28:47.820
I knew you did.

00:28:47.820 --> 00:28:55.190
But there’s so many different ways we can
use these core components to create beautiful,

00:28:55.190 --> 00:29:01.650
new interpretations that helps us understand
these sort of aspects of our histories a little

00:29:01.650 --> 00:29:02.650
more.

00:29:02.650 --> 00:29:12.220
Is there a way that these machines, this kind
of access that creativity has to those ambiguous

00:29:12.220 --> 00:29:16.980
places, is that something you’re all working
towards?

00:29:16.980 --> 00:29:27.490
If we develop these tools farther, if you
can treat it more like a garden, you know

00:29:27.490 --> 00:29:33.140
rather than painting like the individual strokes
on a canvas where you’re controlling it

00:29:33.140 --> 00:29:37.390
in the sense that you put a certain amount
of water on a plant, you choose what you plant

00:29:37.390 --> 00:29:41.710
and how much fertilizer to give it or maybe
you’re really compulsive and you do like

00:29:41.710 --> 00:29:45.790
bonsai, you know the cutting of all the leaves
and stuff.

00:29:45.790 --> 00:29:50.990
But your control is more high level, and part
of the interesting part is letting the system

00:29:50.990 --> 00:29:53.550
do whatever it is that it’s going to do.

00:29:53.550 --> 00:29:57.400
And you understand the system, you go out
each day to your garden and you interact with

00:29:57.400 --> 00:29:59.770
it as the things grow.

00:29:59.770 --> 00:30:02.990
So I sort of think of AI gardens in that same
sense.

00:30:02.990 --> 00:30:07.490
There’s various elements that you want control
over and there’s various elements that you

00:30:07.490 --> 00:30:08.490
don’t.

00:30:08.490 --> 00:30:12.850
And another thing that these computational
creativity algorithms give you is a sense

00:30:12.850 --> 00:30:14.370
of safety in a way.

00:30:14.370 --> 00:30:18.890
So you can explore parts of the creative space
that you might be afraid of a little bit,

00:30:18.890 --> 00:30:23.110
because you have this kind of algorithm that’ll
guarantee that it’ll work out.

00:30:23.110 --> 00:30:27.320
It’s a little bit like GPS, it allows you
to explore parts of the physical world and

00:30:27.320 --> 00:30:28.970
you know you can get home.

00:30:28.970 --> 00:30:34.090
So that’s another thing that really helps
me when I use these creative technologies,

00:30:34.090 --> 00:30:38.300
pushes me a little outside my normal boxes
and puts me in different boxes but they’re

00:30:38.300 --> 00:30:42.550
kind of somewhere else and I know it’ll
work out.

00:30:42.550 --> 00:30:43.830
Actually I have a remark about that.

00:30:43.830 --> 00:30:47.180
I feel the exact opposite about that.

00:30:47.180 --> 00:30:52.830
As I think about collaborating with in a very
performative way, this robotic arm whether

00:30:52.830 --> 00:30:58.150
it’s trained on my own style which Generation
Two is.

00:30:58.150 --> 00:30:59.950
Or it’s just sort of mimicking my movements.

00:30:59.950 --> 00:31:10.050
There’s a real randomness and sort of sense
of unpredictability to it, and lack of understanding

00:31:10.050 --> 00:31:16.310
involved that it’s kind of exhilarating
and exciting but it is not safe, it doesn’t

00:31:16.310 --> 00:31:19.490
feel safe or comforting at all, it’s like
staring into the void.

00:31:19.490 --> 00:31:28.540
Well you know even among humans, the phenomenon
of apophenia where you have two dispirit,

00:31:28.540 --> 00:31:35.420
unrelated bits of stimulus, the brain will
try to make some connections where maybe there

00:31:35.420 --> 00:31:37.440
was no intention to begin with.

00:31:37.440 --> 00:31:41.460
But a lot of artists Peter, make a lot of
hay with that.

00:31:41.460 --> 00:31:47.620
Sure, and central to our kind of creativity
is taking known components and constructing

00:31:47.620 --> 00:31:48.620
new things from them.

00:31:48.620 --> 00:31:52.690
So I can say to you for example: imagine an
egg with wings that’s flying

00:31:52.690 --> 00:31:56.940
around the statue of liberty holding an ice
cream cone instead of a torch.

00:31:56.940 --> 00:31:57.980
And you can do that.

00:31:57.980 --> 00:32:00.250
I could’ve just said anything.

00:32:00.250 --> 00:32:06.130
And so these mental operations seem to take
place in this aspect of our consciousness

00:32:06.130 --> 00:32:09.920
that is this internal virtual reality that
we may call imagination.

00:32:09.920 --> 00:32:13.970
So consciousness has this sort of duo role,
telling us about the world and our bodies

00:32:13.970 --> 00:32:17.510
in it but also this internal virtual reality.

00:32:17.510 --> 00:32:23.320
And this is really central to what we do and
in order to that I think we have to have experience

00:32:23.320 --> 00:32:26.170
in the real world with real things.

00:32:26.170 --> 00:32:32.280
Actually, you know, in some of my work, we’ve
looked at the mental operations, for example,

00:32:32.280 --> 00:32:35.380
rotating a physical object this way or this
way.

00:32:35.380 --> 00:32:40.110
And then we can decode if someone’s imagining
rotating it this way or this way.

00:32:40.110 --> 00:32:44.450
Probably because some similar brain circuits
are involved in actually imagining it in your

00:32:44.450 --> 00:32:48.010
internal virtual reality, which you learned
in the real world.

00:32:48.010 --> 00:32:55.170
There’s a program in France actually Sony
France, maybe Jesse you’ve heard this where

00:32:55.170 --> 00:33:01.430
they fed every Beatles song into this computer
so that the computer could learn how the Beatles

00:33:01.430 --> 00:33:02.950
wrote their songs.

00:33:02.950 --> 00:33:09.840
And then they asked the computer to generate
its own Beatles like songs and you know, they

00:33:09.840 --> 00:33:19.080
had human lyricists.

00:33:19.080 --> 00:33:37.650
If 

00:33:37.650 --> 00:33:43.770
you grew up on the Beatles it’s just like
“eh, alright, that’s kind of cute.”

00:33:43.770 --> 00:33:50.110
If you didn’t grow up on the Beatles though,
I don’t know what your response might, might

00:33:50.110 --> 00:33:52.740
be something like, “oh my gosh, I’ve never
hear anything like this.

00:33:52.740 --> 00:33:55.190
This is brilliant.”

00:33:55.190 --> 00:34:04.190
So the idea… you’re absolutely right it
takes a conscious receptor to decide whether

00:34:04.190 --> 00:34:09.001
something… and so this gets us into the
whole question of aesthetics and taste, whether

00:34:09.001 --> 00:34:11.409
something is in fact creative or not.

00:34:11.409 --> 00:34:15.859
Whether something is creative in the good
sense is going to be a human consciousness

00:34:15.859 --> 00:34:18.159
that says, “oh, that taste good actually.”

00:34:18.159 --> 00:34:24.290
And if my algorithm says well this should
taste good and I just, actually no, it comes

00:34:24.290 --> 00:34:26.700
down to my conscious experience.

00:34:26.700 --> 00:34:30.490
And computers at least the currently configured
are not conscious.

00:34:30.490 --> 00:34:36.569
Our creativity is very much rooted in our
own conscious experience of a world and so

00:34:36.569 --> 00:34:41.850
based on my own conscious experience of the
world, I form mental models, so I know what

00:34:41.850 --> 00:34:44.329
it is for you to feel pain because I’ve
felt pain.

00:34:44.329 --> 00:34:48.980
And we’re constantly modeling things that
are actually not in the input.

00:34:48.980 --> 00:34:54.519
I’m modeling what’s going on in your mind,
based on what’s going on in my mind, that’s

00:34:54.519 --> 00:34:55.649
not visible.

00:34:55.649 --> 00:35:00.890
And so a lot of our creativity is rooted in
our mental models which in return is rooted

00:35:00.890 --> 00:35:07.460
in our consciousness and so I’ll voice a
note of skepticism because in the absence

00:35:07.460 --> 00:35:14.950
of mental models that are rooted in conscious
experience, robots and AI systems I think

00:35:14.950 --> 00:35:18.349
will at best approach as if creativity.

00:35:18.349 --> 00:35:24.920
I actually think that’s one realm in which
the arena of competitive games gets a lot

00:35:24.920 --> 00:35:29.359
of traction in machine learning, is because
there is a set of constraints, there is a

00:35:29.359 --> 00:35:31.509
winner and loser and styles.

00:35:31.509 --> 00:35:38.289
So in that way, it creates a platform around
discussion that’s a little bit…

00:35:38.289 --> 00:35:42.420
it’s more stable than a conversation about
aesthetics.

00:35:42.420 --> 00:35:47.819
Do you feel like the realm of competitive
games and creating these models that could

00:35:47.819 --> 00:35:51.369
be conscious, do you think that’s the right
approach?

00:35:51.369 --> 00:35:57.130
Well when IBM created Deep Blue it could beat
Kasparov.

00:35:57.130 --> 00:36:02.349
But it was doing a brute force search through
zillions of possibilities.

00:36:02.349 --> 00:36:06.670
Whereas Kasparov would instantly see a pattern
so our brain is doing something very different

00:36:06.670 --> 00:36:11.300
and it could beat him, just like an airplane
can fly faster than a bird but it’s doing

00:36:11.300 --> 00:36:14.900
something very different from a bird, in this
case the human brain.

00:36:14.900 --> 00:36:21.131
Well I want to take the, just for fun, the
contrary point of view on this because, so

00:36:21.131 --> 00:36:26.359
a lot like you would use the Kasparov example
right, and there’s recently been these great

00:36:26.359 --> 00:36:32.150
successes of this AlphaGo Program beating
the world champions in Go, and the way that

00:36:32.150 --> 00:36:37.180
they do it is not through brute force search
because that’s, it’s just computational

00:36:37.180 --> 00:36:41.480
intractable because the game of Go is much
more complex.

00:36:41.480 --> 00:36:47.289
But they do it through the creation of these
functions to represent sort of intuition,

00:36:47.289 --> 00:36:51.411
you know to represent like roughly how good
do I feel this move is, and I can’t tell

00:36:51.411 --> 00:36:56.130
you exactly why I feel this good, but I have
this function that I’ve been learning that

00:36:56.130 --> 00:37:00.839
says if these pieces are here and these pieces
are here, then moving here is a good move.

00:37:00.839 --> 00:37:06.089
And it’s because I played a whole bunch
of games and I usually win when I put my piece

00:37:06.089 --> 00:37:08.509
here and all those pieces are in those places.

00:37:08.509 --> 00:37:13.749
And similarly, a lot of the really cutting
edge machine learning and reinforcement learning

00:37:13.749 --> 00:37:19.170
algorithms explicitly have modeling of…
you know there’s some numbers present that

00:37:19.170 --> 00:37:22.809
are saying okay this is my representation
of the environment around me.

00:37:22.809 --> 00:37:28.170
This is my representation of what this other
actor is doing; this is a representation of

00:37:28.170 --> 00:37:31.650
what of what I am doing as an actor in the
system.

00:37:31.650 --> 00:37:39.829
So the concept that our creative critique
or, you know analysis is somehow a fundamentally

00:37:39.829 --> 00:37:43.260
a human thing that requires consciousness.

00:37:43.260 --> 00:37:50.010
I just find it to be a little bit of a small
definition because here you have, you can,

00:37:50.010 --> 00:37:55.990
it’s very easy to imagine agents that can
do things similar in nature to what we might

00:37:55.990 --> 00:37:59.500
consider to be consciousness, but it’s not
human consciousness.

00:37:59.500 --> 00:38:06.849
It’s just sort of a broader definition on
how a thing interacts with its environment.

00:38:06.849 --> 00:38:14.269
So you can have a computer critic and computer
artist that make art that’s optimized for

00:38:14.269 --> 00:38:19.369
what a computer likes, but it’s not necessarily
optimized for what we want.

00:38:19.369 --> 00:38:26.019
It doesn’t, just because it’s a possibility
doesn’t mean, I mean I think we’re always

00:38:26.019 --> 00:38:33.260
going to lend toward creating systems that
you know we enjoy interacting with, that benefit

00:38:33.260 --> 00:38:37.579
us as a society and benefit us in our own
expression.

00:38:37.579 --> 00:38:44.539
Does it seem like there are limits, Peter
you should weigh in as well, to human creativity

00:38:44.539 --> 00:38:48.690
and is there a limit to what artificial creativity
might be?

00:38:48.690 --> 00:38:53.480
Yeah, so you can figure out what’s the maximum
speed a bird can fly through a forest without

00:38:53.480 --> 00:38:54.480
crashing.

00:38:54.480 --> 00:38:58.890
So right now the bird is going pretty slow
he can navigate sufficiently quickly and avoid

00:38:58.890 --> 00:39:02.769
all the obstacles and then land on the tree.

00:39:02.769 --> 00:39:06.849
But if he tries to go too fast he’ll crash
into the tree which is what I think happens

00:39:06.849 --> 00:39:08.049
in the video.

00:39:08.049 --> 00:39:12.510
And that’s exactly the fundamental speed
limit of a flight through forest.

00:39:12.510 --> 00:39:17.920
And there’s a similar fundamental speed
limit for communication, and what we’ve

00:39:17.920 --> 00:39:21.569
been trying to find and what we seem to be
finding is there’s a fundamental speed limit

00:39:21.569 --> 00:39:23.200
to creativity as well.

00:39:23.200 --> 00:39:27.619
There’s a fundamental tension, you can’t
be too novel in a given domain and you can’t

00:39:27.619 --> 00:39:29.859
be too high quality in a domain.

00:39:29.859 --> 00:39:31.890
There’s a tension between the two.

00:39:31.890 --> 00:39:37.230
So it’s easy to be very novel and low quality
just put random stuff together.

00:39:37.230 --> 00:39:41.980
It’s very easy to be high quality and low
novelty just look it up in your favorite cookbook

00:39:41.980 --> 00:39:43.310
or wherever it is.

00:39:43.310 --> 00:39:48.339
But doing both simultaneously is very difficult
and that’s where the limit is.

00:39:48.339 --> 00:39:53.660
And the larger the inspiration set, the known
stuff, the harder it is to be creative.

00:39:53.660 --> 00:39:57.830
So if there are people in the audience who
want to be creative, you should start a new

00:39:57.830 --> 00:40:02.140
field where there’s much more low hanging
fruit actually.

00:40:02.140 --> 00:40:09.079
Are there limits do you think to human creativity
as Lav was suggesting there are to computational

00:40:09.079 --> 00:40:10.079
creativity?

00:40:10.079 --> 00:40:14.319
I don’t know, I mean humans have proven
to be incredibly creative both in the good

00:40:14.319 --> 00:40:20.030
sense of making airplanes for flight or for
murder.

00:40:20.030 --> 00:40:24.500
I think of our human imagination as the greatest
tool and also the greatest weapon we have

00:40:24.500 --> 00:40:31.369
because we can imagine the most amazing things
and also the most horrible things.

00:40:31.369 --> 00:40:34.089
Like the holocaust, that was an act of imagination
actually.

00:40:34.089 --> 00:40:43.789
And my hope is that these tools will eventually
foster human flourishing and not be used to,

00:40:43.789 --> 00:40:47.650
you know also create new ways of murdering
people.

00:40:47.650 --> 00:40:51.050
And I think one question that I would have
for everybody is; what is the goal of all

00:40:51.050 --> 00:40:52.050
this?

00:40:52.050 --> 00:40:54.210
Is it just simply to make new artifacts or
is it…

00:40:54.210 --> 00:40:59.079
I mean when I was a kid there was the Jetsons
and they had this robot and you would say

00:40:59.079 --> 00:41:00.400
“robot do the dishes.”

00:41:00.400 --> 00:41:02.490
And it would do the dishes.

00:41:02.490 --> 00:41:11.650
But is it to make a general-purpose intelligence
that would effectively be a slave?

00:41:11.650 --> 00:41:16.249
My guess is in order to make a general-purpose
robot like the Jetsons robot, it would have

00:41:16.249 --> 00:41:21.220
to be conscious so it would understand what
I mean when I say change the tires.

00:41:21.220 --> 00:41:25.590
I have to have a whole model of a cars and
everything.

00:41:25.590 --> 00:41:29.990
But if it’s conscious, then we have the
problem of slavery again because it would

00:41:29.990 --> 00:41:31.980
suffer.

00:41:31.980 --> 00:41:38.359
So consciousness implies self-awareness, awareness
of self as suppose to another or others.

00:41:38.359 --> 00:41:45.309
The having of experience like pain, like seeing.

00:41:45.309 --> 00:41:50.819
Could a robot be conscious without feeling
pain?

00:41:50.819 --> 00:41:57.440
I suppose I mean there are actually humans
that don’t feel pain and they can see so

00:41:57.440 --> 00:41:58.500
yeah.

00:41:58.500 --> 00:41:59.559
Sociopaths…

00:41:59.559 --> 00:42:02.750
Emotional pain, yeah.

00:42:02.750 --> 00:42:05.800
Jesse what about at your end of things?

00:42:05.800 --> 00:42:11.599
Yeah, I mean I think maybe another way of
thinking about it rather than just… it’s

00:42:11.599 --> 00:42:17.079
like it’s really nice to reflect it back
on our own human experience.

00:42:17.079 --> 00:42:23.760
As we’ve developed tools to better control
mechanical systems, we really began to better

00:42:23.760 --> 00:42:25.930
understand how our bodies work.

00:42:25.930 --> 00:42:32.460
But what’s amazing about these new systems
is they do a math that’s not human but it’s

00:42:32.460 --> 00:42:38.730
similar in a way that can help us provide
introspections in terms of what are my mental

00:42:38.730 --> 00:42:39.730
processes?

00:42:39.730 --> 00:42:45.829
When I’m being self critical, what are the
dynamics of that, how can I interrupt that

00:42:45.829 --> 00:42:46.829
process?

00:42:46.829 --> 00:42:48.079
And how does that affect my creative output?

00:42:48.079 --> 00:42:56.180
You can get introspection in a quantitative
way that can help give you words cause whenever

00:42:56.180 --> 00:42:57.180
you talk…

00:42:57.180 --> 00:43:00.820
I live in the bay area, whenever I talk about
this I just sound like a hippie.

00:43:00.820 --> 00:43:06.329
It’s nice to have things that you can point
to to say hey look this is actual physical

00:43:06.329 --> 00:43:08.170
systems here.

00:43:08.170 --> 00:43:12.460
There something here that cuts common across
human experience that we can have a vocabulary

00:43:12.460 --> 00:43:13.460
to talk about.

00:43:13.460 --> 00:43:19.080
I think to that point too it’s important
to ask why we’re having this conversation

00:43:19.080 --> 00:43:24.040
now and I think part of that, if I can flipped
that script a little bit, is we’re generating

00:43:24.040 --> 00:43:31.480
all this data that’s being collected that’s
very, very difficult to wrap our brains around.

00:43:31.480 --> 00:43:39.249
And these mental models help us understand
the sort of complexity and the scale of our

00:43:39.249 --> 00:43:47.190
own behavior as a collective world and I think
that’s another goal, potentially of continuing

00:43:47.190 --> 00:43:50.260
to research these new areas of cognition.

00:43:50.260 --> 00:43:55.309
So let me ask you to take one more trip to
the table there.

00:43:55.309 --> 00:44:01.569
You gave us a demonstration before of some
of the elemental, the basic gestures that

00:44:01.569 --> 00:44:05.910
this machine, this system is capable of.

00:44:05.910 --> 00:44:11.269
What if you tried to do something more complex?

00:44:11.269 --> 00:44:20.359
So I think what I had here to show was sort
of more on the concept of creating tools that

00:44:20.359 --> 00:44:23.070
actual musicians can use to explore.

00:44:23.070 --> 00:44:30.450
Like we had that novelty sound of the cat/flute
for example, and so what I’ve done here

00:44:30.450 --> 00:44:36.980
on my computer is to create a… we want to
get this into the hands of artist and musician

00:44:36.980 --> 00:44:42.329
and to close the feedback loop cause creativity
takes two to tango, there’s the tool and

00:44:42.329 --> 00:44:45.960
the person and so we want to get that feedback
loop closed.

00:44:45.960 --> 00:44:51.029
So we created this plug in for this popular
software called Ableton Live that if you’ve

00:44:51.029 --> 00:44:58.460
ever gone to see any electronic music, most
likely it was made for Ableton Live.

00:44:58.460 --> 00:45:02.099
So here what we can do that instead of just
interpolating between just two instruments

00:45:02.099 --> 00:45:10.109
we can create an entire grid of instruments
and allow a musician to take sort of a random

00:45:10.109 --> 00:45:14.470
walk, take a walk through this space and explore
a whole variety of sounds.

00:45:14.470 --> 00:45:19.170
So what I can do is rather than just play
this with my hand I can just sort of play

00:45:19.170 --> 00:45:20.170
a clip here.

00:45:20.170 --> 00:45:28.890
So right now the instrument is like an organ
sound and you can see this is the software

00:45:28.890 --> 00:45:36.710
that we have going but you see we’ve created
a manifold for people to play on where we

00:45:36.710 --> 00:45:40.160
can actually just drag through this space.

00:45:40.160 --> 00:45:47.190
And see the evolution of sound as it goes
between different instruments and some of

00:45:47.190 --> 00:45:48.190
the spots...

00:45:48.190 --> 00:45:50.690
all of a sudden we’re in a dubstep thing.

00:45:50.690 --> 00:45:58.210
But you can see the spots where it’s in
between the different sounds.

00:45:58.210 --> 00:46:03.960
And it creates a whole bunch of different
textures…

00:46:03.960 --> 00:46:08.410
alright that might be the start to my next
track, or something like that.

00:46:08.410 --> 00:46:16.740
It gives you ability to interact in this space
that if you were trying to design by hand

00:46:16.740 --> 00:46:20.609
to be able to draw through those things it’d
be very difficult to do.

00:46:20.609 --> 00:46:23.410
Sort of one of those things.

00:46:23.410 --> 00:46:29.349
Sougwen, where do you see computational creativity
headed in the visual arts?

00:46:29.349 --> 00:46:32.789
Are there sort of analogous tools available
to you?

00:46:32.789 --> 00:46:41.420
Yea definitely there a lot of style transfers
and this sort of visual assist…

00:46:41.420 --> 00:46:48.239
style transfer is sort of the neural networks
reading the visual pixel data essentially

00:46:48.239 --> 00:46:54.640
of an image, a popular painting for instance
and then extracting that style, like Starry

00:46:54.640 --> 00:47:00.150
Night or what have you, and then transferring
that into your next selfie or a picture of

00:47:00.150 --> 00:47:04.069
your dog or something.

00:47:04.069 --> 00:47:07.009
Yeah, it’s compelling.

00:47:07.009 --> 00:47:13.799
So I think there’s that as a very common
approach to using these systems in the visual

00:47:13.799 --> 00:47:14.799
arts now.

00:47:14.799 --> 00:47:22.690
What I get really excited about is ways in
which we can create new forms of collaboration

00:47:22.690 --> 00:47:28.539
through sort of processes that we’re training
these neural nets on.

00:47:28.539 --> 00:47:38.329
What about the visual arts as performative
arts where normally we only see the sausage.

00:47:38.329 --> 00:47:43.880
We don’t see the grinding and all that but
it seems like with what you’re doing you

00:47:43.880 --> 00:47:45.650
want us to see some of the process.

00:47:45.650 --> 00:47:46.650
Very much so.

00:47:46.650 --> 00:47:52.640
I think for me the creative process is much
more important than the outcome and that’s

00:47:52.640 --> 00:47:59.630
why drawing as a collaboration, that’s why
it is drawing and not dance.

00:47:59.630 --> 00:48:08.819
I think music performances is a little more
ambiguous, but actually being able to explore

00:48:08.819 --> 00:48:15.769
the drawn mark in a way that isn’t representational
could be a reflection of consciousness or

00:48:15.769 --> 00:48:23.559
sort of exploratory intentionality and doing
that alongside a, I hesitate to say artificial

00:48:23.559 --> 00:48:31.270
intelligence, but machine learning system
approaches an understanding or at least could

00:48:31.270 --> 00:48:38.279
create a data set that could help us approach
an understanding of what the exploratory creative

00:48:38.279 --> 00:48:39.279
process is.

00:48:39.279 --> 00:48:48.160
Well Jesse you know music throughout history
really has had a kind of ambivalent reaction

00:48:48.160 --> 00:48:51.539
to new technologies, “oh my gosh digital
music.

00:48:51.539 --> 00:48:53.410
All the musicians will be put out of work.

00:48:53.410 --> 00:48:54.989
Oh my gosh the pianos.

00:48:54.989 --> 00:49:01.680
Al the harpsichordist will be put out of work.”

00:49:01.680 --> 00:49:07.329
And yet we still have real musicians, we still
have harpsichordists even.

00:49:07.329 --> 00:49:17.460
So the idea that there’s room for a whole
new kind of generation of creative artist

00:49:17.460 --> 00:49:26.599
in this field, it’s kind of a new idea,
but it seems like it has analogies to things

00:49:26.599 --> 00:49:28.030
that have gone before.

00:49:28.030 --> 00:49:30.130
Right in some ways it’s a very old idea.

00:49:30.130 --> 00:49:34.339
That’s where I was sort of going that this
is kind of just part of a long coevolution

00:49:34.339 --> 00:49:39.200
that’s existed between technology and music
in this case.

00:49:39.200 --> 00:49:45.170
I think it really just takes a generation
that really owns it.

00:49:45.170 --> 00:49:52.339
They don’t feel attached partially to the
things that came before, because they weren’t

00:49:52.339 --> 00:49:55.270
alive when that was really happening so why
should they feel attached to it?

00:49:55.270 --> 00:50:01.599
Some are traditional, I play jazz but I also
like pushing it in new directions.

00:50:01.599 --> 00:50:07.210
So I think that will always happen, there
will always be people find strength in the

00:50:07.210 --> 00:50:13.509
continuity of learning that harpsichord from
the 1600’s and those types of things.

00:50:13.509 --> 00:50:17.490
Alright you mentioned the word ownership and
it gets up to something I said before I want

00:50:17.490 --> 00:50:19.680
to come back to, that is agency.

00:50:19.680 --> 00:50:29.210
So Lav you help create and your team helps
to create this entity that comes up with new

00:50:29.210 --> 00:50:32.989
fashion ideas, new food recipes, new metal
alloys.

00:50:32.989 --> 00:50:35.989
Who owns that?

00:50:35.989 --> 00:50:41.519
So the current understanding of US trademark
and copyright patent laws that it goes into

00:50:41.519 --> 00:50:46.950
the public domain because there has to be
a human inventor to claim ownership.

00:50:46.950 --> 00:50:50.430
Machines don’t have property rights, at
least currently.

00:50:50.430 --> 00:50:56.130
And so that’s the difficulty, it creates
this perverse incentive for people to pretend

00:50:56.130 --> 00:51:01.839
like they were very fundamentally involved
in the creative process.

00:51:01.839 --> 00:51:06.930
But it also raises the national question,
how should we think about property rights

00:51:06.930 --> 00:51:09.569
in this area?

00:51:09.569 --> 00:51:14.460
And it’s actually important because who
will fund the computational creative research

00:51:14.460 --> 00:51:19.430
if you can’t actually patent using those
tools eventually?

00:51:19.430 --> 00:51:23.519
So there’s no backwards flow of incentive
essentially.

00:51:23.519 --> 00:51:26.999
So the nice thing let’s say pencils is they’re
considered researchable.

00:51:26.999 --> 00:51:31.150
So it’s not the inventor of the pencil that
gets property rights on things that are invented

00:51:31.150 --> 00:51:32.630
using pencils.

00:51:32.630 --> 00:51:37.650
But when you do invent using a pencil you
can claim property rights on that.

00:51:37.650 --> 00:51:43.700
Right so similarly we need some incentives
perhaps, a revision of this understanding

00:51:43.700 --> 00:51:47.900
to encourage development of new computational
creativity technologies.

00:51:47.900 --> 00:51:49.079
Peter?

00:51:49.079 --> 00:51:53.229
I also think we need wisdom in handling tools.

00:51:53.229 --> 00:51:56.470
Any tool can be a two-edged sword, a hammer
could be used to murder somebody or build

00:51:56.470 --> 00:52:00.400
a house, even an atom bomb can deflect an
asteroid.

00:52:00.400 --> 00:52:06.009
But these tools are so powerful that they
can kind of take on a life of their own and

00:52:06.009 --> 00:52:07.009
that I think is a danger.

00:52:07.009 --> 00:52:09.269
And I think there’s an example of that in
everyone’s pocket, right.

00:52:09.269 --> 00:52:10.279
These cell phones are amazing.

00:52:10.279 --> 00:52:16.609
You have all the information available in
the universe with Wikipedia right there.

00:52:16.609 --> 00:52:19.670
But it becomes very distracting and many people…

00:52:19.670 --> 00:52:26.140
I was walking across the Dartmouth green and
the sky was on fire with the red sunset and

00:52:26.140 --> 00:52:29.140
everybody, a hundred people were doing this.

00:52:29.140 --> 00:52:33.489
And I almost wanted to say people look at
this, but I didn’t have the courage to do

00:52:33.489 --> 00:52:34.489
it.

00:52:34.489 --> 00:52:40.550
I noted that in my mind thirty years ago,
walking across that Dartmouth green, I would

00:52:40.550 --> 00:52:45.589
see ten people holding hands and I don’t
see that hardly anymore.

00:52:45.589 --> 00:52:51.099
And these devices meant to foster human connectivity
have in many ways undermined it because people

00:52:51.099 --> 00:52:54.819
are more distracted, less attentive, here
instead of talking to people.

00:52:54.819 --> 00:53:01.530
Like when I was a kid and AI is very powerful,
but it runs the same risk, we have to own

00:53:01.530 --> 00:53:02.530
it.

00:53:02.530 --> 00:53:06.499
We have to become agentic ourselves and make
sure it serves our ends creative or other

00:53:06.499 --> 00:53:10.299
wise and that we don’t become slaves to
it.

00:53:10.299 --> 00:53:16.539
But if AI does reach a point Jesse, Sougwen,
where it can compose a piece of music, create

00:53:16.539 --> 00:53:22.059
a genuine work of visual art, something that
moves us that genuinely passes the Turing

00:53:22.059 --> 00:53:27.220
test, that we know where it came from and
it uplifts us anyway.

00:53:27.220 --> 00:53:28.990
You don’t care.

00:53:28.990 --> 00:53:37.569
Then does that AI entity have some sort of
agency, does it have some sort of ownership

00:53:37.569 --> 00:53:44.660
as opposed to the humans who set it in motion
maybe a generation earlier?

00:53:44.660 --> 00:53:51.479
(pause) We’re in William Gibson territory…science
fiction

00:53:51.479 --> 00:53:52.479
I mean…

00:53:52.479 --> 00:53:53.789
it’s all good stuff.

00:53:53.789 --> 00:54:00.150
I think what you were saying about cell phones
and stuff, it’s really fun to think about

00:54:00.150 --> 00:54:05.500
these scenarios where you start thinking about
personification and then constant evolution

00:54:05.500 --> 00:54:06.500
and stuff.

00:54:06.500 --> 00:54:11.680
But the fact of the matter is for technology
to ever reach that point, we’ll have gone

00:54:11.680 --> 00:54:16.730
through so many transformative things in our
society…

00:54:16.730 --> 00:54:21.000
like there’s lower hanging fruit than that
that will change everything.

00:54:21.000 --> 00:54:25.599
And so I always think, like there’s lots
of groups like Google, open AI, DeepMind that

00:54:25.599 --> 00:54:31.609
are really focused on more of the short term
things of like how does this affect our society

00:54:31.609 --> 00:54:37.109
here and now and if we ever reach that point,
the only way we’ll ever do that if we figure

00:54:37.109 --> 00:54:40.640
out all the stuff that came before it because
otherwise it just won’t happen.

00:54:40.640 --> 00:54:45.570
But can I add that in order to get to that
point, we’ll have to have deep mental models

00:54:45.570 --> 00:54:50.950
on what it means to be human being rooted
in what we have experience as being a human

00:54:50.950 --> 00:54:51.950
being.

00:54:51.950 --> 00:54:55.269
So for example you know that scene in Les
Mis where Jean Valjean is in prison for nineteen

00:54:55.269 --> 00:54:59.660
years and he hates the system and he’s angry
and he stays with this priest.

00:54:59.660 --> 00:55:05.019
He then steals the silverware of the priest,
and the police catch him and bring him in.

00:55:05.019 --> 00:55:12.460
And the priest in a act of true Christian
grace says to him release him, he didn’t

00:55:12.460 --> 00:55:13.460
steal it.

00:55:13.460 --> 00:55:15.569
I gave it to him and here you forgot the platter.

00:55:15.569 --> 00:55:21.130
And we find that deeply moving and beautiful
because we can understand having hit bottom.

00:55:21.130 --> 00:55:25.999
And then by having hit bottom he goes through
a mental transformation and he realizes I’m

00:55:25.999 --> 00:55:30.180
not this, I need to become a new kind of person.

00:55:30.180 --> 00:55:35.980
That can only be understood by us because
we have human experience and I’m skeptical

00:55:35.980 --> 00:55:39.809
that AI is going to be able to understand
redemption like that.

00:55:39.809 --> 00:55:46.299
But maybe then again if we get to that point
they will be humans or very close to us.

00:55:46.299 --> 00:55:50.279
Sougwen… tough act to follow there.

00:55:50.279 --> 00:55:58.660
No, I think part of how we even categorize
what makes us human is how we create our own

00:55:58.660 --> 00:55:59.660
narratives.

00:55:59.660 --> 00:56:06.099
Like you explained this sort of profoundly
human moment through the narrative of Les

00:56:06.099 --> 00:56:07.099
Mis.

00:56:07.099 --> 00:56:13.380
I think right now we’re in the process of
creating narratives around machine learning,

00:56:13.380 --> 00:56:17.319
we’re calling it artificial intelligence,
we’re calling it designed intelligence.

00:56:17.319 --> 00:56:23.009
This is definitely an interesting precursor
to that question of agency.

00:56:23.009 --> 00:56:28.160
We form our own through creating narratives
and now we’re able to co-create narratives

00:56:28.160 --> 00:56:29.160
with these machines.

00:56:29.160 --> 00:56:32.799
So I think that’s sort of the path forward.

00:56:32.799 --> 00:56:39.390
The arc of human history has been to acknowledge
agency for more and more people.

00:56:39.390 --> 00:56:48.059
Agency was for many generations was restricted
to a certain gender and certain group of people

00:56:48.059 --> 00:56:57.619
and we hope that’s a continually expanding
definition of agency and whether it can include

00:56:57.619 --> 00:56:59.200
things that are artificial.

00:56:59.200 --> 00:58:27.450
I guess that’s a question that will be answered
down the road.

